var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"kf/#Kalman-Filters","page":"Kalman Filters","text":"This tutorial covers the standard Kalman Filter (KF), its nonlinear extensions (EKF and UKF), and the Rauch–Tung–Striebel (RTS) smoother on a simple kinematic mock benchmark.","title":"Kalman Filters"},{"category":"section","location":"kf/#Standard-KF","page":"Kalman Filters","text":"We consider a linear kinematic model with position, velocity, and acceleration as state variables:\n\nusing MeteoModels\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Random\n\nRandom.seed!(42)\n\nn = 3   # state: [position, velocity, acceleration]\nm = 1   # we observe only position\nnt = 100\n\n# Time step\nδ = 0.1\n\n# Process noise covariance (acceleration-driven)\nσ_acc = 0.02\nQ = [δ^2/2;δ;1] * [δ^2/2 δ 1] * σ_acc^2\nnoise = Noise(Q)\n\n# Transition model: constant-acceleration kinematics\nF = [1 δ δ^2/2;0 1 δ;0 0 1]\ntransition = Model(F)\n\n# Observation noise and model: observe only position\nσ_obs = 1.0\nR = σ_obs^2 * I(m)\nobs_noise = Noise(R)\nH = [1.0 0.0 0.0]\nobservation = Model(H)\n\nGenerate a ground-truth trajectory and noisy observations:\n\nx0 = [1.0,1.0,1.0]\nΣ0 = Matrix(I(n))\nprior = SecondMoment(x0,Σ0)\n\ntrue_transition = Model(F)\ntrue_history = execute(true_transition,prior,1:nt)\ntrue_states = collect_forecasted_states(true_history)\nobs = build_observations(observation,true_states,obs_noise)\n\nConstruct and run the filter:\n\nkf = KalmanFilter(transition,observation,prior;noise,obs_noise)\nresults = loop(kf,obs)\nvisualise(true_states,results)","title":"Standard KF"},{"category":"section","location":"kf/#Extended-Kalman-Filter-(EKF)","page":"Kalman Filters","text":"For nonlinear dynamics, pass a function to Model instead of a matrix.  The filter detects the NonlinearModel trait and linearises automatically at each step:\n\n# Nonlinear transition: elementwise square, then kinematic step\nfunction f_nl(x)\n    x_sq = x .^ 2\n    F * x_sq\nend\n\n# Nonlinear observation: sum of state components\nh_nl(x) = [sum(x)]\n\ntransition_nl = Model(f_nl)   # NonlinearModel → triggers EKF\nobservation_nl = Model(h_nl)\n\nprior_nl = SecondMoment(x0,Σ0)\nekf = KalmanFilter(transition_nl,observation_nl,prior_nl;noise,obs_noise)\nresults_ekf = loop(ekf,obs)\nvisualise(true_states,results_ekf)","title":"Extended Kalman Filter (EKF)"},{"category":"section","location":"kf/#Unscented-Kalman-Filter-(UKF)","page":"Kalman Filters","text":"The UKF propagates a set of carefully chosen sigma points through the nonlinear operators instead of relying on explicit linearisation.  Wrap the prior in SigmaPoints:\n\nprior_ukf = SigmaPoints(SecondMoment(x0,Σ0))\nukf = KalmanFilter(transition_nl,observation_nl,prior_ukf;noise,obs_noise)\nresults_ukf = loop(ukf,obs)\nvisualise(true_states,results_ukf)","title":"Unscented Kalman Filter (UKF)"},{"category":"section","location":"kf/#RTS-Smoother","page":"Kalman Filters","text":"After a forward filter pass, the RTS smoother runs a backward pass to refine all posterior estimates using future observations.  The convenience function smooth_loop combines both passes:\n\nkf2 = KalmanFilter(transition,observation,SecondMoment(x0,Σ0);noise,obs_noise)\nsmooth_results = smooth_loop(kf2,obs)\nvisualise(true_states,smooth_results)\n\nFor manual control, use smoothen! after obtaining loop results:\n\nkf3 = KalmanFilter(transition,observation,SecondMoment(x0,Σ0);noise,obs_noise)\nfilter_results = loop(kf3,obs)\n\nsmooth_history = similar(filter_results.state_history)\nsmoothen!(smooth_history,kf3,filter_results.state_history)","title":"RTS Smoother"},{"category":"section","location":"bias_aware/#Bias-Aware-Kalman-Filter","page":"Bias-Aware Filter","text":"Observation systems often suffer from systematic bias — a persistent offset between the true signal and what the sensor reports.  BiasAwareKalmanFilter corrects this online by training an EchoStateNetwork (ESN) to predict the innovation bias and incorporating its Jacobian into the Kalman gain.","title":"Bias-Aware Kalman Filter"},{"category":"section","location":"bias_aware/#Reservoir-Computing-Background","page":"Bias-Aware Filter","text":"An Echo State Network is a recurrent neural network whose internal (reservoir) weights are fixed at random initialisation; only the linear readout layer W_textout is trained.  The hidden state evolves as\n\ns_t+1 = (1 - alpha)s_t + alphatanh(rho W s_t + sigma W_textin x_t)\n\nand the output is y_t = W_textout^top s_t.","title":"Reservoir Computing Background"},{"category":"section","location":"bias_aware/#Training-an-ESN-on-Innovation-Data","page":"Bias-Aware Filter","text":"The idea is to pre-train an ESN to map the current innovation tildey_t to the next innovation tildey_t+1.  During filtering, the ESN predicts the bias carried by the innovation and adjusts the analysis step accordingly.\n\nusing MeteoModels\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\nusing Random\n\nRandom.seed!(42)\n\n# ── Lorenz-63 data generation ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nfunction lorenz63!(du,u,p,_)\n    du[1] = p[1] * (u[2] - u[1])\n    du[2] = u[1] * (p[2] - u[3]) - u[2]\n    du[3] = u[1] * u[2] - p[3] * u[3]\nend\n\np = (10.0,28.0,8/3)\ndt = 0.01\nprob = ODEProblem(lorenz63!,[1.0,0.0,0.0],(0.0,200.0),p)\nsol = solve(prob,Tsit5();dt,saveat=dt)\ndata = reduce(hcat,sol.u)   # 3 × 20001\n\nn_input = 3\nshift = 300\nn_train = 5000\n\ninput_data = data[:,shift:(shift + n_train - 1)]\ntarget_data = data[:,(shift + 1):(shift + n_train)]\n\nConstruct and train the ESN:\n\nnstate = 300\nwashout = 30\nλ = 1e-6\n\nesn = EchoStateNetwork(n_input,nstate,n_input;\n    radius=0.9,\n    connect=5,\n    scaling=0.1,\n    modifier_in=DoNotModify(),\n    modifier_state=DoNotModify(),\n)\n\nmethod = TrainRecurrentNeuralNetwork(;\n    augmentation=NoAugmentation(),\n    regularisation=NoRegularisation(),\n    washout,\n    λ,\n)\n\ntrain(method,esn,input_data,target_data)\n\nVerify closed-loop forecast quality:\n\nn_predict = 1250\ntest_data = data[:,(shift + n_train + 1):(shift + n_train + n_predict)]\n\ny_pred = forecast(esn,1:n_predict)   # closed-loop from current ESN state","title":"Training an ESN on Innovation Data"},{"category":"section","location":"bias_aware/#Hyper-parameter-Tuning-via-Recycle-Validation","page":"Bias-Aware Filter","text":"RecycleValidation performs cross-validated grid search over spectral radius and input scaling without extra data:\n\nradius_range = range(0.7,1.1,length=6)\nscaling_range = range(1e-4,0.5,length=6)\n\nrv_method = RecycleValidation(method,radius_range,scaling_range;\n    Nfolds=4,\n    Ntrain=n_train,\n    Nvalidation=50,\n)\n\ntrain(rv_method,esn,input_data,target_data)\n\nTo jointly search over the Tikhonov regularisation lambda as well:\n\ntikhonov = [1e-8,1e-6,1e-4]\nrv_tikhonov = RecycleValidation(method,tikhonov,radius_range,scaling_range;\n    Nfolds=4,Ntrain=n_train,Nvalidation=50)\ntrain(rv_tikhonov,esn,input_data,target_data)","title":"Hyper-parameter Tuning via Recycle Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"bias_aware/#Wrapping-a-KF-with-Bias-Awareness","page":"Bias-Aware Filter","text":"After training the ESN, pass it to BiasAwareKalmanFilter as the bias model. The first maxiter steps run a standard warm-up pass to initialise the ESN state before bias correction is activated:\n\n# ── Filter setup (biased observations: constant offset added to H*x) ─────────\n\nn = 3\nH = Float64.(I(n))\nobservation = Model(H)\ntransition = Model(0.9 * I(n))   # simple contractive linear model for illustration\n\nQ = 0.01 * I(n)\nR = 0.05 * I(n)\nx0 = [1.0,0.0,0.0]\nΣ0 = 0.1 * I(n)\n\nnoise = Noise(Q)\nobs_noise = Noise(R)\nprior = SecondMoment(x0,Σ0)\n\nkf = KalmanFilter(transition,observation,prior;noise,obs_noise)\n\n# ── Wrap with bias-aware correction ──────────────────────────────────────────\n\nbkf = BiasAwareKalmanFilter(kf,esn;γ=10,maxiter=50)\n\n# Generate biased observations\ntrue_x = execute(transition,SecondMoment(x0,Σ0),1:200)\nx_mat = reduce(hcat,collect_forecasted_states(true_x))\nbias = fill(0.3,n)   # systematic offset\nobs_biased = x_mat .+ bias .+ 0.05 * randn(n,200)\n\nresults = loop(bkf,obs_biased)\n\nThe bias-aware filter is composable with any inner KalmanFilter subtype, including AdaptiveKalmanFilter and InflationKalmanFilter — see the Composability tutorial.","title":"Wrapping a KF with Bias Awareness"},{"category":"section","location":"enkf/#Ensemble-Kalman-Filter","page":"Ensemble Kalman Filter","text":"This tutorial demonstrates the EnKF on two benchmarks: a rainfall–runoff model and the chaotic Lorenz-96 system.","title":"Ensemble Kalman Filter"},{"category":"section","location":"enkf/#Rainfall–runoff","page":"Ensemble Kalman Filter","text":"using MeteoModels\nusing Distributions\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Random\n\nRandom.seed!(42)\n\nn = 3    # state size\nm = 1    # observation size\nne = 30   # ensemble size\nnt = 50   # time steps\ndt = 1.0\ntimes = dt:dt:nt*dt\n\nDefine the true dynamics and generate ground-truth data:\n\nR = 0.5^2 * Float64.(I(m))\nobs_noise = Noise(R)\n\nfunction true_transition_fn(states)\n    rainfall = clamp.(rand(Uniform(0,20),n) .- 10.0,0.0,10.0)\n    evapcoef = rand(Uniform(0.05,0.1),n)\n    x = states + rainfall - evapcoef .* states\n    map(x -> clamp(x,0.0,50.0),x)\nend\n\nfunction observation_fn(states)\n    sum(sqrt.(map(x -> clamp(x,0.0,50.0),states)),dims=1)\nend\n\ntrue_transition = Model(true_transition_fn)\nx0_true = rand(Uniform(20,40),n)\nprior_true = build_prior(x0_true)\n\ntrue_history = execute(true_transition,prior_true,times)\ntrue_states = collect_forecasted_states(true_history)\nobservation = Model(observation_fn)\ntrue_obs = build_observations(observation,true_states,obs_noise)\n\nDefine the model transition (with intentional model error) and run the EnKF:\n\nfunction transition_fn(states)\n    rainfall = clamp.(rand(Uniform(0,20),n) .- 10.0,0.0,10.0)\n    evapcoef = rand(Uniform(0.05,0.1),n)\n    x = 1.01 .* states .^ 0.99 .+ 1.02 .* rainfall .- evapcoef .* states\n    map(x -> clamp(x,0.0,50.0),x)\nend\n\ntransition = Model(transition_fn)\nensemble = rand(Uniform(10,50),n,ne)\nprior = build_prior(ensemble)\nenkf = KalmanFilter(transition,observation,prior;obs_noise)\n\nresults = loop(enkf,true_obs)\nvisualise(true_states,results)\n\nThe default Ensemble prior uses the EnKFStrategy (stochastic perturbed-observations variant).  To use the deterministic DEnKF instead:\n\nprior_d = build_prior(ensemble;strategy=DEnKFStrategy())\ndenkf = KalmanFilter(transition,observation,prior_d;obs_noise)\nresults_d = loop(denkf,true_obs)","title":"Rainfall–runoff"},{"category":"section","location":"enkf/#Lorenz-96","page":"Ensemble Kalman Filter","text":"The Lorenz-96 system models a scalar meteorological quantity along a latitude circle:\n\ndoty_i = (y_i+1 - y_i-2)y_i-1 - y_i + 8 quad i = 1dots40\n\nwith cyclic boundary conditions y_0 = y_40, y_-1 = y_39, y_41 = y_1.\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\n\nn = 40\nne = 50\nm = 20    # observe every second variable\ndt = 0.01\nnspin = 1000\nnt = 100\n\nSpin up from a perturbed fixed point to reach the attractor:\n\nconst F96 = 8.0\n\nfunction lorenz96!(dx,x,_,_)\n    n = length(x)\n    @inbounds for i in 1:n\n        dx[i] = (x[mod1(i+1,n)] - x[mod1(i-2,n)]) * x[mod1(i-1,n)] - x[i] + F96\n    end\nend\n\nx0_spin = fill(F96,n); x0_spin[n÷2] += 0.001\nprob_spin = ODEProblem(lorenz96!,x0_spin,(0.0,nspin*dt))\nsol_spin = solve(prob_spin,Tsit5();dt,adaptive=false,saveat=(nspin-1)*dt:dt:nspin*dt)\nx0_true = sol_spin.u[end]\n\nGenerate true trajectory and observations:\n\nt0 = nspin*dt\ntf = t0 + nt*dt\ntrue_transition = Model(ODEWrapper(Tsit5(),lorenz96!,copy(x0_true),t0+dt:dt:tf,nothing))\n\ngrid = stencil((t0,tf),dt)\ntrue_history = execute(true_transition,grid)\ntrue_states = collect_forecasted_states(true_history)\n\n# Observe every second variable\nH = zeros(m,n)\nfor i in 1:m;H[i,2i-1] = 1.0;end\nobservation = Model(H)\n\nR96 = 0.5^2 * I(m)\nobs_noise96 = Noise(R96)\nobs = build_observations(observation,true_states,obs_noise96)\nobs_on_grid = expand(obs,stencil((t0,tf),dt),grid)\n\nBuild the ensemble from perturbed initial conditions and run the EnKF:\n\nx0_ens = x0_true .+ 0.1 * randn(n,ne)\nprior96 = build_prior(x0_ens)\n\ntransition96 = Model(ODEWrapper(Tsit5(),lorenz96!,copy(x0_ens),t0+dt:dt:tf,nothing))\nenkf96 = KalmanFilter(transition96,observation,prior96;obs_noise=obs_noise96)\n\nresults96 = loop(enkf96,obs_on_grid)\nvisualise(true_states,results96)\n\n(Image: Lorenz benchmark)","title":"Lorenz-96"},{"category":"section","location":"high_level/#High-Level-API-and-Native-Integration-with-SciML-and-Gridap-ecosystems","page":"High-Level API & Native Integrations","text":"MeteoModels.jl provides a high-level API built around TimeStencils, which partitions a simulation window into named phases (warm-up, training, washout, spread, data assimilation).  All core functions (execute, warmup!, loop, etc.) accept either a plain vector of time points or a TimeStencils object.","title":"High-Level API and Native Integration with SciML and Gridap ecosystems"},{"category":"section","location":"high_level/#TimeStencils","page":"High-Level API & Native Integrations","text":"TimeStencils divides a continuous time interval into up to six named sub-windows:\n\nPhase constant Meaning\nWARMUP model spin-up (prior advances, no DA)\nTRAIN reservoir / ESN training window\nWASHOUT ESN washout (transient discarded)\nSPREAD ensemble spread build-up\nDA data assimilation window\nALL entire interval\n\nusing MeteoModels\nusing LinearAlgebra\n\nn = 10\nne = 20\ndt = 0.1\n\nts = TimeStencils(;\n    dt,\n    t0=0.0,\n    t_warmup=5.0,    # 50 warm-up steps\n    t_da=10.0,   # 100 DA steps\n)\n\n# Individual stencils (vectors of time points):\nts[WARMUP]   # 50-element range\nts[DA]       # 100-element range\nts[ALL]      # 150-element range","title":"TimeStencils"},{"category":"section","location":"high_level/#Warm-up-and-Forecasting","page":"High-Level API & Native Integrations","text":"F = 0.9 * Matrix(I(n))\nH = zeros(1,n); H[1,1] = 1.0\ntransition = Model(F)\nobservation = Model(H)\nobs_noise = Noise(0.01^2 * I(1))\n\nvals0 = randn(n,ne)\nprior = build_prior(copy(vals0))\nenkf = KalmanFilter(transition,observation,prior;obs_noise)\n\n# Advance the filter prior through the warm-up window (in-place, no history):\nwarmup!(enkf,ts)   # uses WARMUP phase by default\n\n# Forward forecast with full history stored as a StencilArray:\nsa = execute(transition,build_prior(copy(vals0)),ts)\n\n# Extract phase-specific history:\nwarmup_history = forecasted_history(sa,WARMUP)   # Vector{<:Law}\nda_history = forecasted_history(sa,DA)\n\n# Just the state matrices:\nwarmup_states = collect_forecasted_states(sa,WARMUP)   # Vector{<:AbstractMatrix}\nda_states = collect_forecasted_states(sa,DA)\n\n# Mean across ensemble at the end of the DA window:\nμ_end = collect_forecasted_mean(sa,DA) |> last","title":"Warm-up and Forecasting"},{"category":"section","location":"high_level/#Building-Observations-from-a-Trajectory","page":"High-Level API & Native Integrations","text":"build_observations applies the observation model column-by-column to a state history:\n\ntrue_transition = Model(0.95 * I(n))\ntrue_prior = build_prior(randn(n))\ntrue_history = execute(true_transition,true_prior,ts[ALL])\ntrue_states = collect_forecasted_states(true_history)\n\nobs = build_observations(observation,true_states,obs_noise)\nobs_on_grid = expand(obs,stencil(ts[DA]),ts[DA])\n\nFor ensemble trajectories (3-D output):\n\nobs_3d = build_3d_observations(observation,collect_forecasted_states(sa,DA))","title":"Building Observations from a Trajectory"},{"category":"section","location":"high_level/#SciML-Integration-—-ODE-Models","page":"High-Level API & Native Integrations","text":"Any SciML-compatible ODE can be wrapped with ODEWrapper and passed to Model. The integrator is advanced one step per evaluate! call, making ODE models drop-in replacements for algebraic transition models:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\n\n# Scalar decay: du/dt = -u  →  u(t) = exp(-t)\ndecay!(du,u,_,_) = (du[1] = -u[1])\nu0_ode = [1.0]\nt_range = dt:dt:20.0\n\node_model = Model(ODEWrapper(Tsit5(),decay!,u0_ode,t_range,nothing;\n    solver_kwargs=(adaptive=false,)))\n\nprior_ode = build_prior(copy(u0_ode))\node_history = execute(ode_model,prior_ode,ts[DA])\node_states = collect_forecasted_states(ode_history)\n\nMemoryModel wraps any model so it reuses its internal integrator state across calls, which is essential for advancing a persistent ODE without restarting each step:\n\nup = MemoryModel(ode_model)\nwarmup!(up,copy(prior_ode),ts[WARMUP])","title":"SciML Integration — ODE Models"},{"category":"section","location":"high_level/#Ensemble-ODE-Transition","page":"High-Level API & Native Integrations","text":"For an ensemble filter, initialise the ODEWrapper with the ensemble matrix:\n\nn_ode = 3\nne = 30\n\nfunction lorenz63!(du,u,p,_)\n    du[1] = p[1]*(u[2] - u[1])\n    du[2] = u[1]*(p[2] - u[3]) - u[2]\n    du[3] = u[1]*u[2] - p[3]*u[3]\nend\n\np63 = (10.0,28.0,8/3)\nx0_ens = randn(n_ode,ne)\nt0_ens = 0.0\ntf_ens = 50.0\nt_range = dt:dt:tf_ens\n\node_transition = Model(ODEWrapper(Tsit5(),lorenz63!,copy(x0_ens),t_range,p63))\nH63 = Float64.(I(n_ode))\nobs_noise63 = Noise(0.1^2 * I(n_ode))\nprior63 = build_prior(copy(x0_ens))\nenkf63 = KalmanFilter(ode_transition,Model(H63),prior63;obs_noise=obs_noise63)","title":"Ensemble ODE Transition"},{"category":"section","location":"high_level/#FEM–Reduced-Basis-Integration","page":"High-Level API & Native Integrations","text":"For PDE-constrained problems, MeteoModels.jl integrates with GridapROMs.jl via TransientPDEModel, which wraps a Gridap ODESolution as a transition model:\n\n# Assume `ode_sol` is a GridapROMs ODEParamSolution and `FESpace` is defined\n# pde_model = TransientPDEModel(ode_sol)\n# prior_pde = build_prior(get_free_dof_values(x0_FE))\n# enkf_pde  = KalmanFilter(pde_model,observation,prior_pde;obs_noise)\n# results   = loop(enkf_pde,obs)\n\nFor parameter identification from PDE outputs, ADParamIdentification wraps a parametric AffineFEStateMap and minimises the weighted observation misfit ell(mu) = R^-12(mathcalH(u(mu)) - y)^2 via Zygote AD:\n\n# ad = ADParamIdentification(μ_to_u,u_to_ℓ,pspace,u_to_obs,obs_noise)\n# result = identify_parameter(ad,obs;iterations=500)\n# μ_opt  = Optim.minimizer(result)\n\nSee the test suite (test/ParamODEs.jl, test/TransientParamPDEs.jl) for complete working examples with actual Gridap/GridapROMs setups.","title":"FEM–Reduced Basis Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"adaptive/#Adaptive,-Inflation,-and-Localisation-Filters","page":"Adaptive & Inflation","text":"This tutorial shows how to wrap a base Kalman filter with covariance inflation, spatial localisation, and online Q/R adaptation — each requiring just a one-line change relative to the standard KalmanFilter call.\n\nAll examples use the Lorenz-96 system as in the EnKF tutorial.","title":"Adaptive, Inflation, and Localisation Filters"},{"category":"section","location":"adaptive/#Setup","page":"Adaptive & Inflation","text":"using MeteoModels\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\nusing Distributions\n\nn = 40\nne = 50\nm = 20\ndt = 0.01\nnspin = 1000\nnt = 100\nconst F96 = 8.0\n\nfunction lorenz96!(dx,x,_,_)\n    n = length(x)\n    @inbounds for i in 1:n\n        dx[i] = (x[mod1(i+1,n)] - x[mod1(i-2,n)]) * x[mod1(i-1,n)] - x[i] + F96\n    end\nend\n\nx0_spin = fill(F96,n); x0_spin[n÷2] += 0.001\nsol_spin = solve(ODEProblem(lorenz96!,x0_spin,(0.0,nspin*dt)),Tsit5();dt,adaptive=false)\nx0_true = sol_spin.u[end]\n\nt0 = nspin*dt\ntf = t0 + nt*dt\n\ntrue_transition = Model(ODEWrapper(Tsit5(),lorenz96!,copy(x0_true),t0+dt:dt:tf,nothing))\ngrid = stencil((t0,tf),dt)\ntrue_history = execute(true_transition,grid)\ntrue_states = collect_forecasted_states(true_history)\n\nH = zeros(m,n); for i in 1:m;H[i,2i-1] = 1.0;end\nobservation = Model(H)\nobs_noise = Noise(0.5^2 * I(m))\nobs = build_observations(observation,true_states,obs_noise)\nobs_on_grid = expand(obs,stencil((t0,tf),dt),grid)\n\nx0_ens = x0_true .+ 0.1 * randn(n,ne)\n\nfunction make_enkf()\n    transition = Model(ODEWrapper(Tsit5(),lorenz96!,copy(x0_ens),t0+dt:dt:tf,nothing))\n    prior = build_prior(copy(x0_ens))\n    KalmanFilter(transition,observation,prior;obs_noise)\nend","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"adaptive/#Multiplicative-Covariance-Inflation","page":"Adaptive & Inflation","text":"MultInflation multiplies the forecast covariance by a constant factor rho  1 before computing the Kalman gain, preventing ensemble collapse in highly nonlinear regimes:\n\nenkf_infl = InflationKalmanFilter(make_enkf(),MultInflation(1.05))\nresults_infl = loop(enkf_infl,obs_on_grid)\nvisualise(true_states,results_infl)\n\nAlternatively, construct directly (same keyword arguments as KalmanFilter):\n\nenkf_infl2 = InflationKalmanFilter(\n    Model(ODEWrapper(Tsit5(),lorenz96!,copy(x0_ens),t0+dt:dt:tf,nothing)),\n    observation,\n    build_prior(copy(x0_ens));\n    obs_noise,\n    inflation = MultInflation(1.05)\n)","title":"Multiplicative Covariance Inflation"},{"category":"section","location":"adaptive/#Adaptive-NLL-Inflation","page":"Adaptive & Inflation","text":"NLLInflation selects the inflation factor online by minimising the Negative Log-Likelihood of the observations at each step, within a bounded interval:\n\nnll = NLLInflation(bounds=(1.0,2.0),tolerance=1e-4)\nenkf_nll = InflationKalmanFilter(make_enkf(),nll)\nresults_nll = loop(enkf_nll,obs_on_grid)\nvisualise(true_states,results_nll)","title":"Adaptive NLL Inflation"},{"category":"section","location":"adaptive/#Covariance-Localisation","page":"Adaptive & Inflation","text":"Localisation tapers the sample covariance to suppress spurious long-range correlations. TaperModel wraps a taper function with a distance metric and a radius:\n\ntaper_model = TaperModel(n;taper=GaspariCohn(),distance=ℓ1)\nenkf_loc = LocalisationKalmanFilter(make_enkf(),taper_model)\nresults_loc = loop(enkf_loc,obs_on_grid)\nvisualise(true_states,results_loc)\n\nAvailable tapers: GaspariCohn (default), GaussianTaper, BickelLevina, Cai.   Available distances: ℓ1, ℓ2, geostrophic.","title":"Covariance Localisation"},{"category":"section","location":"adaptive/#Online-Q/R-Adaptation","page":"Adaptive & Inflation","text":"AdaptiveKalmanFilter estimates the process noise Q and observation noise R online via an EM-like innovation-based update with exponential moving average step step:\n\nenkf_adapt = AdaptiveKalmanFilter(make_enkf();step=0.1,nblocks=1)\nresults_adapt = loop(enkf_adapt,obs_on_grid)\nvisualise(true_states,results_adapt)\n\nThe nblocks parameter controls the block structure of the Q decomposition: 1 uses a rank-1 (scalar) Q, while larger values allow richer spatial covariance patterns.","title":"Online Q/R Adaptation"},{"category":"section","location":"adaptive/#Localisation-Inflation-(combined)","page":"Adaptive & Inflation","text":"InflationKalmanFilter accepts a LocalisationKalmanFilter as its inner filter:\n\nenkf_loc_infl = InflationKalmanFilter(\n    LocalisationKalmanFilter(make_enkf(),taper_model),\n    MultInflation(1.05)\n)\nresults_loc_infl = loop(enkf_loc_infl,obs_on_grid)\nvisualise(true_states,results_loc_infl)","title":"Localisation + Inflation (combined)"},{"category":"section","location":"composability/#Composability","page":"Composability","text":"All filter wrappers in MeteoModels.jl implement the same KalmanFilter interface, so they can be freely composed.  Each wrapper adds exactly one capability and delegates everything else to its filter field.","title":"Composability"},{"category":"section","location":"composability/#The-Wrapper-Hierarchy","page":"Composability","text":"The composition order matters: outer wrappers call inner ones via their interface methods, so the innermost filter holds the base covariance logic.\n\nA typical pattern for a challenging large-scale problem:\n\nBiasAwareKalmanFilter\n    └── AdaptiveKalmanFilter\n            └── InflationKalmanFilter\n                    └── LocalisationKalmanFilter\n                            └── EnsembleKalmanFilter  (base)","title":"The Wrapper Hierarchy"},{"category":"section","location":"composability/#Example:-Localisation-Inflation-Adaptation","page":"Composability","text":"using MeteoModels\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\n\nn = 40\nne = 50\nm = 20\ndt = 0.01\n\nfunction lorenz96!(dx,x,_,_)\n    n = length(x)\n    @inbounds for i in 1:n\n        dx[i] = (x[mod1(i+1,n)] - x[mod1(i-2,n)]) * x[mod1(i-1,n)] - x[i] + 8.0\n    end\nend\n\n# (assume true_states, obs_on_grid, obs_noise already built — see adaptive.md)\n\nx0_ens = randn(n,ne)\nt0, tf = 10.0, 11.0\n\nfunction base_enkf()\n    transition = Model(ODEWrapper(Tsit5(),lorenz96!,copy(x0_ens),t0+dt:dt:tf,nothing))\n    prior = build_prior(copy(x0_ens))\n    H = zeros(m,n); for i in 1:m;H[i,2i-1] = 1.0;end\n    observation = Model(H)\n    obs_noise = Noise(0.5^2 * I(m))\n    KalmanFilter(transition,observation,prior;obs_noise)\nend\n\ntaper_model = TaperModel(n;taper=GaspariCohn(),distance=ℓ1)\n\n# Three wrappers stacked:\nf = AdaptiveKalmanFilter(\n        InflationKalmanFilter(\n            LocalisationKalmanFilter(base_enkf(),taper_model),\n            MultInflation(1.05)\n        );\n        step=0.1\n    )\n\nresults = loop(f,obs_on_grid)\nvisualise(true_states,results)","title":"Example: Localisation + Inflation + Adaptation"},{"category":"section","location":"composability/#Adding-Bias-Awareness","page":"Composability","text":"After the ESN is trained (see bias_aware.md), wrapping is a one-liner:\n\nesn = EchoStateNetwork(m,200,m;radius=0.9,scaling=0.1,\n    modifier_in=DoNotModify(),modifier_state=DoNotModify())\n# ... train esn ...\n\nf_biased = BiasAwareKalmanFilter(f,esn;γ=10,maxiter=50)\nresults_b = loop(f_biased,obs_on_grid)","title":"Adding Bias Awareness"},{"category":"section","location":"composability/#DVar-Variational-Methods","page":"Composability","text":"ThreeDVar and FourDVar are standalone minimisers (not wrappers) that solve the variational analysis problem via BFGS optimisation.  They are complementary to the ensemble filters rather than composable with them:\n\n# prior background: x_b with covariance B\nB = 0.1 * I(n)\nxb = zeros(n)\n\nH_lin = zeros(m,n); for i in 1:m;H_lin[i,2i-1] = 1.0;end\nobs_var = Noise(0.5^2 * I(m))\ntransition_lin = Model(0.99 * I(n))   # linearised background model\n\nvar3d = ThreeDVar(transition_lin,Model(H_lin),SecondMoment(xb,B);obs_noise=obs_var)\nresults_3d = loop(var3d,obs_on_grid)","title":"DVar Variational Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"composability/#Interface-Guarantee","page":"Composability","text":"Any KalmanFilter subtype exposes:\n\nget_prior(f) / get_observation_prior(f)\nget_transition_model(f) / get_observation_model(f)\nget_noise(f) / get_observation_noise(f)\nforecast!(posterior, f) / analyse!(posterior, f, obs)\nloop(f, obs) → FilterResults\n\nWriting a new wrapper requires only overriding the steps that differ; everything else falls through to the inner filter automatically.","title":"Interface Guarantee"},{"category":"section","location":"#MeteoModels.jl","page":"Home","text":"Welcome to the documentation for MeteoModels.jl \n\nnote: Note\nThe documentation is currently under construction.","title":"MeteoModels.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Description","page":"Home","text":"This package provides a set of tools for data assimilation and uncertainty quantification for weather forecasting applications. ","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"#Manual","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Manual"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.ADParamIdentification","page":"Home","text":"struct ADParamIdentification\n\nEncapsulates all components needed to identify unknown parameters of a PDE-constrained observation model via gradient-based optimisation with automatic differentiation.\n\nFields:\n\nμ_to_u: an AffineFEStateMap that maps a parameter vector μ to the PDE state u(μ);\nu_to_ℓ: a StateParamMap that evaluates the scalar loss on the weighted innovation;\nu_to_obs: a Model mapping the PDE state to the observation space; may be linear (AlgebraicModel) or nonlinear (NonlinearModel);\npspace: a ParamSpace or TransientParamSpace that defines the parameter domain and supplies the default initial guess;\nweight: the precomputed square-root precision matrix R^-12, stored as a dense matrix so that Zygote's BLAS pullbacks apply during the reverse pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.ADParamIdentification"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.AdaptiveKalmanFilter","page":"Home","text":"struct AdaptiveKalmanFilter{F<:KalmanFilter} <: KalmanFilter\n\nWraps an inner Kalman filter F and estimates the process-noise covariance Q and observation-noise covariance R on-line using an innovation-based EM-like update.\n\nAt each step, the cached transition Jacobian F, observation Jacobian H, prior/posterior covariances, and innovations from the previous step are used to form new estimates hat Q and hat R, which are then blended into the running estimates via an exponential moving average with weight step.\n\nFields:\n\nfilter: the underlying KalmanFilter (can be any concrete subtype);\nlast_posterior: copy of the posterior from the previous analysis step, needed for the adaptive update;\nstep: EMA step size alpha in (01 controlling how fast Q and R adapt;\ncache: pre-allocated workspace for Jacobians, covariances, and innovation history.\n\nConstruct via AdaptiveKalmanFilter(filter; step=0.1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.AdaptiveKalmanFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.AddBias","page":"Home","text":"struct AddBias{A<:Number} <: BiasStyle\n\nAppends a constant value as an extra component to the input vector, increasing its length by one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.AddBias"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.AlgebraicModel","page":"Home","text":"struct AlgebraicModel{T,A<:AbstractMatrix{T}} <: LinearModel\n  matrix::A\nend\n\nStandard implementation of a LinearModel. The field matrix represents the constant  Jacobian of the model itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.AlgebraicModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.BiasAwareKalmanFilter","page":"Home","text":"struct BiasAwareKalmanFilter{A<:KalmanFilter} <: KalmanFilter\n\nWraps an inner Kalman filter A and corrects for systematic observation bias using a RecurrentNeuralNetwork trained online.\n\nDuring a warm-up phase (first maxiter steps) the inner filter runs as-is, accumulating innovations to train the bias model.  Once the counter exceeds maxiter (isaware returns true) the analysis step adjusts the innovation by the current bias estimate before computing the Kalman gain and state update.\n\nFields:\n\nfilter: the underlying KalmanFilter (can be any concrete subtype);\nbias_model: a RecurrentNeuralNetwork that maps the current innovation to a bias vector;\nregularisation: penalty coefficient γ applied to the bias Jacobian in the modified gain;\nawareness: [IterCounter] tracking how many analysis steps have been performed;\ncache: pre-allocated workspace for Jacobians and intermediate matrices.\n\nConstruct via BiasAwareKalmanFilter(f, bias_model; γ=10, maxiter=50) where f is any KalmanFilter instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.BiasAwareKalmanFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.BiasStyle","page":"Home","text":"abstract type BiasStyle end\n\nBase type for bias-appending strategies within a Modifier pipeline. Concrete subtypes: NoBias, AddBias.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.BiasStyle"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.BickelLevina","page":"Home","text":"struct BickelLevina <: TaperFunction end\n\nHard-thresholding taper: returns 1 for ‖x‖ ≤ 1 and 0 elsewhere.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.BickelLevina"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.Cai","page":"Home","text":"struct Cai <: TaperFunction end\n\nPiecewise-linear taper: 1 for |x| ≤ 1/2, linearly decaying to 0 at |x| = 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.Cai"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.DEnKF","page":"Home","text":"const DEnKF{A<:Model,B<:Model,C<:Law,D<:Law,E<:Law,F<:Law} = EnsembleKalmanFilter{A,B,C,D,E,F,DEnKFStrategy}\n\nImplements the DEnKF. Construct by passing an Ensemble with strategy=DEnKFStrategy() as the prior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.DEnKF"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.DEnKFStrategy","page":"Home","text":"struct DEnKFStrategy <: EnsembleStyle end\n\nTrait for ensembles mimicking the DEnKF (deterministic EnKF) method:\n\nrun the forecast step on each ensemble member (see forecast!);\ncompute the Kalman gain K as usual (see kalman_gain!);\ncompute the ensemble innovations ỹ (see innovation!);\nupdate the ensemble mean according to the formula: \n\nμ  μ + K  mean(ỹ)\n\nwhere μ is the ensemble mean;\n\nupdate the ensemble anomaly according to the formula:\n\nA  (I - \frac12 KH) A \n\nwhere H is the jacobian of the observation model, evaluated in the forecasted ensemble mean, and A is the ensemble anomaly. This is the so-called deterministic approximation of DEnKF;\n\nupdate the ensemble E according to the formula:\n\nEi = Ai + μ \n\nfor every i = 1nₑ. The covariance Σ is then estimated via its ensemble counterpart:\n\nΣ = A  Aᵀ  (nₑ - 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.DEnKFStrategy"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.DataAugmentation","page":"Home","text":"abstract type DataAugmentation <: DataTransformation end\n\nBase type for input-augmentation strategies applied to training data before feeding into the reservoir.  Concrete subtypes: NoAugmentation, ScaledAugmentation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.DataAugmentation"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.DataRegularisation","page":"Home","text":"abstract type DataRegularisation <: DataTransformation end\n\nBase type for data-regularisation strategies (e.g. additive noise) applied to training inputs.  Concrete subtypes: NoRegularisation, AdditiveNoiseRegularisation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.DataRegularisation"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.EchoStateNetwork","page":"Home","text":"struct EchoStateNetwork <: RecurrentNeuralNetwork\n\nEcho State Network (ESN) / reservoir computing model.\n\nThe hidden state evolves as\n\ns_t+1 = (1 - alpha) s_t + alpha  f(rho W s_t + sigma W_mathrmin x_t)\n\nwhere f is the activation function, rho is the spectral radius, sigma is the input scaling, and alpha is the leak rate.  The readout y_t = W_mathrmout s_t is linear and fitted by ridge regression via TrainRecurrentNeuralNetwork.\n\nFields:\n\nactivation: element-wise activation (default tanh);\nstate: mutable hidden-state vector s_t;\nweights: reservoir weight matrix (sparse);\nweights_in: input weight matrix;\nweights_out_T: transposed readout matrix (fitted during training);\nmodifier_in, modifier_state: Modifier pipelines for inputs and states;\nradius: mutable spectral-radius reference rho;\nscaling: mutable input-scaling reference sigma;\nleak: leak rate alpha.\n\nConstruct via EchoStateNetwork(ninput, nstate, noutput; kwargs...).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.EchoStateNetwork"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.EnKF","page":"Home","text":"const EnKF{A<:Model,B<:Model,C<:Law,D<:Law,E<:Law,F<:Law} = EnsembleKalmanFilter{A,B,C,D,E,F,EnKFStrategy}\n\nImplements the standard EnKF. Construct by passing an Ensemble with strategy=EnKFStrategy() (the default) as the prior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.EnKF"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.EnKFStrategy","page":"Home","text":"struct EnKFStrategy <: EnsembleStyle end\n\nTrait for ensembles mimicking the EnKF method:\n\nrun the forecast step on each ensemble member (see forecast!);\ncompute the Kalman gain K as usual (see kalman_gain!);\ncompute the ensemble innovations ỹ (see innovation!);\nupdate the ensemble according to the formula:\n\nE  E + K  ỹ \n\nwhere E is the ensemble matrix. The mean μ and covariance Σ are then estimated via their  ensemble counterparts:\n\nμ = ᵢ Ei  nₑ\nΣ = ᵢ (Ei - μ)(Ei - μ)ᵀ  (nₑ - 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.EnKFStrategy"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.EnSRKF","page":"Home","text":"const EnSRKF{A<:Model,B<:Model,C<:Law,D<:Law,E<:Law,F<:Law} = EnsembleKalmanFilter{A,B,C,D,E,F,EnSRKFStrategy}\n\nEnsemble Square-Root Kalman Filter. Deterministic (no perturbed observations), so the ensemble spread is updated via a square-root anomaly formula instead of the stochastic EnKF perturbation.\n\nConstruct by passing an Ensemble with strategy=EnSRKFStrategy() as the prior:\n\nprior = Ensemble(values; strategy=EnSRKFStrategy())\nf = KalmanFilter(transition, observation, prior)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.EnSRKF"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.EnSRKFStrategy","page":"Home","text":"struct EnSRKFStrategy <: EnsembleStyle end\n\nTrait for ensembles using the Ensemble Square-Root Kalman Filter (EnSRKF) method. The filter is deterministic: observations are not perturbed, so the ensemble spread is updated exactly via a square-root factorisation of the innovation covariance rather than by the stochastic perturbation of the standard EnKF.\n\nThe analysis proceeds as follows:\n\nrun the forecast step on each ensemble member (see forecast!);\ncompute the observation anomaly S = H A_f where H is the Jacobian of the observation model and A_f the forecast anomaly;\nassemble the innovation covariance\n\nC = (n_e - 1)R + S S^top\n\nand eigendecompose it as C = Phi Lambda Phi^top;\n\ncompute the mean Kalman gain\n\nK = P_xy C^-1\n\nwhere P_xy is the state–observation cross-covariance;\n\nform E = Lambda^-12 Phi^top S (shape m times n_e) and compute its SVD E = U Sigma V^top;\nupdate the ensemble mean\n\nmu_a = mu_f + K tildey\n\nwhere tildey = z - H mu_f is the mean innovation;\n\nupdate the ensemble anomaly\n\nA_a = A_f cdot V sqrtI - Sigma^topSigma V^top\n\nrebuild each ensemble member as Ei = mu_a + A_ai.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.EnSRKFStrategy"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.Ensemble","page":"Home","text":"struct Ensemble{C<:EnsembleStyle,A<:AbstractMatrix,B<:AbstractVector} <: SecondMoment\n  values::A\n  mean::B \n  anomaly::A\n  strategy::C\nend\n\nThis SecondMoment distribution represents the ensemble needed to run the EnsembleKalmanFilter. Fields:\n\nvalues: n  n_e-dimensional matrix storing the ensemble values;\nmean: n-dimensional vector representing the ensemble mean;\nanomaly: n  n_e-dimensional matrix storing the ensemble anomaly;\nstrategy: trait of type EnsembleStyle which determines the update type  of the ensemble.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.Ensemble"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.EnsembleKalmanFilter","page":"Home","text":"struct EnsembleKalmanFilter{A<:Model,B<:Model,C<:Law,D<:Law,E<:Law,F<:Law,G<:EnsembleStyle} <: KalmanFilter\n\nImplements an Ensemble Kalman Filter. In particular:\n\ninstead of propagating a single probability distribution as in a Kalman filter, we do so for several different (ensemble) distributions.\nthe explicit update of the state covariance matrix is not required. Indeed, the variability of the state is implicitly encoded in the ensemble's spread.\nthe remaining steps are equivalent to a standard Kalman Filter.\n\nThe EnsembleStyle type parameter G is inferred from the strategy of the prior Ensemble at construction time. Subtypes:\n\nEnKF\nDEnKF\nEnSRKF\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.EnsembleKalmanFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.EnsembleStyle","page":"Home","text":"abstract type EnsembleStyle end\n\nTrait specifying how the ensemble covariance of an Ensemble distribution should be updated.  The reason why this is kept as a parameter is that, in ensemble filtering strategies, computing the  ensemble covariance with the usual formula \n\nΣ = ᵢ (Ei - μ)*(Ei - μ)ᵀ  (nₑ - 1)\n\nis generally expensive, and thus alternative strategies are sought. Here, E are the ensemble members.  Subtypes:\n\nEnKFStrategy\nDEnKFStrategy\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.EnsembleStyle"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.Filter","page":"Home","text":"abstract type Filter end\n\nType reserved for filter operators for data processes, such as the Kalman filter (KF). A filter  should be given as input an transition operator, a observation operator, and a prior distribution, which should be representative of the distribution of the initial sample. The prior should be  updated iteratively, assimilating observations at multiple different time instants, as well as  the last bayesian estimate of the (unknown) state variable. Check the function loop  for suitable iterative updates of the prior. Check here for more details on Kalman filtering. Subtypes:\n\nKalmanFilter\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.Filter"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.FilterResults","page":"Home","text":"struct FilterResults\n  state_history::AbstractVector{<:Law}\n  obs_measures::ResultsTable\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.FilterResults"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.FirstMoment","page":"Home","text":"const FirstMoment = Law{1}\n\nType reserved for distributions characterised only by their first moment, i.e. the mean, accessed via the function mean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.FirstMoment"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.FourDVar","page":"Home","text":"struct FourDVar <: DVar\n\nFour-Dimensional Variational data assimilation (4DVar).\n\nExtends ThreeDVar to a time window t_0 t_K by propagating the state forward through a transition model and accumulating observation-error terms at each assimilation time:\n\nJ(x_0) = fracalpha2(x_0 - x_0^b)^top B^-1(x_0 - x_0^b)\n        + fracbeta2sum_k=0^K(mathcalH(x_k) - y_k)^top R^-1(mathcalH(x_k) - y_k)\n\nOptimisation is performed with BFGS over the initial condition x_0.\n\nConstruct via\n\nFourDVar(transition, observation, prior; B=..., R=...)\n\nand run over a window of observations with loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.FourDVar"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.GaspariCohn","page":"Home","text":"struct GaspariCohn <: TaperFunction end\n\nFifth-order piecewise-polynomial taper (Gaspari & Cohn 1999).  Compactly supported on |x| ≤ 1, smooth at the origin, and non-negative everywhere.  The standard default for ensemble covariance localisation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.GaspariCohn"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.GaussianTaper","page":"Home","text":"struct GaussianTaper <: TaperFunction end\n\nGaussian taper exp(-x²/2).  Globally supported (never exactly zero), so it damps but does not hard-threshold long-range correlations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.GaussianTaper"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.GenericFirstMoment","page":"Home","text":"struct GenericFirstMoment{A<:AbstractVector} <: FirstMoment\n  mean::A \nend\n\nMost basic implementation of a FirstMoment distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.GenericFirstMoment"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.GenericKalmanFilter","page":"Home","text":"struct GenericKalmanFilter{A<:Model,B<:Model,C<:Law,D<:Law,E<:Law,F<:Law} <: KalmanFilter\n  transition::A\n  observation::B\n  prior::C\n  obs_prior::D\n  noise::E\n  obs_noise::F\n  cache::KalmanCache\nend\n\nFilter subtype implementing a Kalman filter procedure.  Fields:\n\ntransition: Model representing the transition operator; \nobservation: Model representing the observation operator; \nprior: Law representing the probability distribution for the state; \nobs_prior: Law representing the probability distribution for the observation;\nnoise: Law representing the probability distribution for the process (state) noise;\nobs_noise: Law representing the probability distribution for the observation noise;\ncache: cached object allowing for efficient in-place operations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.GenericKalmanFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.GenericModel","page":"Home","text":"struct GenericModel{F<:FType} <: NonlinearModel\n  form::F\nend\n\nStandard implementation of a NonlinearModel. The field form represents the function or Gridap Map characterising the model itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.GenericModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.InflationKalmanFilter","page":"Home","text":"struct InflationKalmanFilter{A<:KalmanFilter,B<:InflationModel} <: KalmanFilter\n\nWraps an inner Kalman filter A and inflates the forecast covariance before computing the Kalman gain, using the inflation strategy B.\n\nCovariance inflation counteracts ensemble collapse and filter divergence by artificially broadening the prior.  Two strategies are available:\n\nMultInflation: multiplies the covariance by a fixed scalar ρ;\nNLLInflation: adaptively tunes ρ at each step by minimising the negative log-likelihood of the innovation.\n\nFields:\n\nfilter: the underlying KalmanFilter;\ninflation: an InflationModel that supplies (and optionally optimises) the inflation factor;\ncache: stashed prior/obs-prior and optimisation workspace for the adaptive case.\n\nConstruct via InflationKalmanFilter(filter; inflation=MultInflation()) or from raw transition/observation/prior arguments (which automatically includes localisation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.InflationKalmanFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.InflationModel","page":"Home","text":"abstract type InflationModel end\n\nBase type for covariance-inflation strategies used by InflationKalmanFilter. An InflationModel supplies a scalar factor ρ by which the forecast covariance is multiplied before the Kalman gain is computed.\n\nSubtypes:\n\nMultInflation: constant multiplicative inflation;\nNLLInflation: adaptive inflation tuned by negative log-likelihood.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.InflationModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.Law","page":"Home","text":"abstract type Law{N} end\n\nType representing a probability distribution characterised by N moments. Subtypes:\n\nFirstMoment\nSecondMoment\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.Law"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.Linear","page":"Home","text":"struct Linear <: Linearity end\n\nTrait used by LinearModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.Linear"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.LinearModel","page":"Home","text":"const LinearModel = Model{Linear}\n\nModels that are fully characterised by an m  n-dimensional Jacobian matrix J, i.e.\n\nx  Jx\n\nrepresents the action of a LinearModel on an n-dimensional vector x. If the input is a  distribution d, then:\n\nif d is a FirstMoment distribution with mean μ, then the output is a FirstMoment \n\nwith mean Jμ;\n\nd is a SecondMoment distribution with mean μ and covariance Σ, then the output  \n\nis a SecondMoment with mean Jμ, and covariance JPJᵀ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.LinearModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.Linearity","page":"Home","text":"abstract type Linearity end\n\nA Model trait that allows certain optimizations during the evaluation of the models themselves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.Linearity"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.LocalisationKalmanFilter","page":"Home","text":"struct LocalisationKalmanFilter{A<:KalmanFilter} <: KalmanFilter\n\nWraps an inner Kalman filter A and applies covariance localisation after each forecast step via a TaperModel.\n\nLocalisation suppresses spurious long-range correlations that arise in ensemble-based filters when the ensemble size is small relative to the state dimension.  After the transition step the sample covariance (or anomaly matrix) is element-wise multiplied by a distance-decay taper function.\n\nFields:\n\nfilter: the underlying KalmanFilter;\ntaper: a TaperModel that defines the taper function and the distance matrix;\ncache: pre-allocated workspace for the taper computation.\n\nConstruct via LocalisationKalmanFilter(filter; taper=GaspariCohn(), npoints=n) or by passing transition, observation, and prior directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.LocalisationKalmanFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.LogNumber","page":"Home","text":"struct LogNumber{N} <: Real\n\nA real number whose value field is interpreted as a logarithm in base N.\n\nArithmetic operations on two LogNumber{N} values act on the stored exponents directly, so a * b adds exponents, a / b subtracts them, etc.  This lets hyper-parameter grids be expressed in log-space while interoperating with plain Real arithmetic.\n\nAliases: Log = LogNumber{ℯ}, Log2 = LogNumber{2}, Log10 = LogNumber{10}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.LogNumber"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.MemoryModel","page":"Home","text":"struct MemoryModel{A<:Linearity} <: Model{A}\n\nA Model wrapper that pre-allocates and reuses the evaluation cache across calls.\n\nUnlike a bare model — which allocates a fresh cache on every evaluate! call — a MemoryModel holds a persistent cache and prior that are reused.  This makes it suitable for models (e.g. ODEModel) whose cache encapsulates integrator state that must be preserved between steps.\n\nFields:\n\nmodel: the wrapped Model;\nprior: the input distribution, updated in-place on each call;\ncache: the persistent evaluation cache returned by return_cache(model, prior).\n\nConstruct via MemoryModel(model, prior).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.MemoryModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.Model","page":"Home","text":"abstract type Model{A<:Linearity} <: Map end\n\nType used for operator-like quantities, such as functions or Gridap Maps. For performance  reasons, we distinguish models depending on their Linearity trait. To evaluate a Model a  in a point x — usually an n-dimensional vector or a scalar — simply call\n\na(x)\n\nwhich automatically triggers the function evaluate(a,x), originally defined in Gridap, and overwritten here a few times. For in-place evaluations, use instead the syntax\n\nevaluate!(cache,a,x)\n\nwhere cache = return_cache(a,x) is a suitable cached object. \n\nThe main characteristic of a Model is that it may also be evaluated in a probability distribution.  Given an input Law prior, the output\n\nposteriori = a(priori)\n\nreturns another distribution posteriori, which should be thought of the propagation of priori through the model a. The type of Model and input Law determine the expression of posterior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.Model"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.Modifier","page":"Home","text":"struct Modifier{A<:NormaliseStyle,B<:TransformStyle,C<:BiasStyle} <: DataTransformation\n\nComposable pre-processing pipeline applied to reservoir inputs or states.  Sequentially applies normalisation → transformation → bias appending.\n\nConstruct via Modifier(; normalisation, transformation, bias) or use DoNotModify for the identity pipeline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.Modifier"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.MultInflation","page":"Home","text":"struct MultInflation <: InflationModel\n\nConstant multiplicative covariance inflation.  The forecast covariance is scaled by the fixed factor ρ at every analysis step.\n\nFields:\n\nρ: inflation factor (default 1.0, i.e. no inflation).\n\nConstruct via MultInflation(ρ) or MultInflation() for the identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.MultInflation"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.NLLInflation","page":"Home","text":"struct NLLInflation <: InflationModel\n\nAdaptive multiplicative covariance inflation that tunes the factor ρ at every analysis step by minimising the negative log-likelihood of the innovation.\n\nThe optimal ρ is found by a scalar line search over [lower, upper].  The iteration stops when the reduction in NLL falls below tolerance.\n\nFields:\n\nbounds: (lower, upper) box constraint on ρ (defaults: (1e-3, 10.0));\ntolerance: stopping criterion for the inner NLL optimisation (default 1e-1);\nρ: mutable reference to the current inflation factor (reset to -1.0 each step).\n\nConstruct via NLLInflation(; lower=1e-3, upper=10.0, tolerance=1e-1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NLLInflation"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.NLLInflationKalmanFilter","page":"Home","text":"const NLLInflationKalmanFilter{A<:KalmanFilter} = InflationKalmanFilter{A,<:NLLInflation}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NLLInflationKalmanFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.NoAugmentation","page":"Home","text":"struct NoAugmentation <: DataAugmentation end\n\nIdentity augmentation: returns the input unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NoAugmentation"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.NoBias","page":"Home","text":"struct NoBias <: BiasStyle end\n\nIdentity bias: returns the input unchanged (no extra component appended).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NoBias"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.NoNormalisation","page":"Home","text":"struct NoNormalisation <: NormaliseStyle end\n\nIdentity normalisation: returns the input unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NoNormalisation"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.NoRegularisation","page":"Home","text":"struct NoRegularisation <: DataRegularisation end\n\nIdentity regularisation: returns the input unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NoRegularisation"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.NoTransformation","page":"Home","text":"struct NoTransformation <: TransformStyle end\n\nIdentity transformation: returns the input unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NoTransformation"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.Nonlinear","page":"Home","text":"struct Nonlinear <: Linearity end\n\nTrait used by NonlinearModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.Nonlinear"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.NonlinearModel","page":"Home","text":"const NonlinearModel = Model{Nonlinear}\n\nModels that are in general characterised by either a function, or a Gridap Map. Denoting  such function/Map by by f, the action of a NonlinearModel on an n-dimensional vector x is  simply defined by\n\nx  f(x)\n\nwhere the output is an m-dimensional vector, or a scalar. If the input is a distribution d, then:\n\nif d is a FirstMoment distribution with mean μ, then the output is a FirstMoment \n\nwith mean f(μ);\n\nd is a SecondMoment distribution with mean μ and covariance Σ, then the output  \n\nis a SecondMoment with mean f(μ), and covariance whose definition depends on the types of  boht a and d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NonlinearModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.NormalLaw","page":"Home","text":"struct NormalLaw{A<:AbstractVector,B<:AbstractMatrix} <: SecondMoment\n  mean::A \n  covariance::B\nend\n\nNormal distribution of mean mean and covariance covariance; a SecondMoment distribution defaults to a NormalLaw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NormalLaw"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.Normalisation","page":"Home","text":"struct Normalisation{A<:AbstractVector} <: NormaliseStyle\n\nElement-wise normalisation: divides each component x[i] by factor[i].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.Normalisation"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.NormaliseStyle","page":"Home","text":"abstract type NormaliseStyle end\n\nBase type for normalisation strategies within a Modifier pipeline. Concrete subtypes: NoNormalisation, Normalisation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NormaliseStyle"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.ODEModel","page":"Home","text":"struct ODEModel{A} <: DifferentialModel\n\nWraps an ODEWrapper (an ODE integrator) as a NonlinearModel.\n\nEach call to evaluate! advances the integrator by one step from the current state, making it suitable as the transition model in a filter operating on ODE-governed systems. Construct via Model(sol::ODEWrapper).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.ODEModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.RTS","page":"Home","text":"struct RTS <: Smoother\n\nRauch–Tung–Striebel (RTS) smoother.\n\nGiven the sequence of filter posteriors produced by loop, runs a backward pass that refines each estimate using information from all future observations.  For a linear-Gaussian model the RTS smoother is the exact fixed-interval smoother.\n\nUse via smooth_loop to obtain smoothed histories directly, or call smoothen! on an existing filter history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.RTS"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.RecurrentNeuralNetwork","page":"Home","text":"abstract type RecurrentNeuralNetwork <: NeuralNetwork end\n\nBase type for recurrent neural networks.  Subtypes must implement get_state (internal hidden state) and get_output (current readout).  The main concrete subtype is EchoStateNetwork.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.RecurrentNeuralNetwork"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.RecycleValidation","page":"Home","text":"struct RecycleValidation{A<:TrainMethod,B<:UpdateRule} <: TrainMethod\n\nTraining method that wraps another TrainMethod and tunes hyper-parameters via cross-validation on held-out forecast windows (the \"recycle\" trick).\n\nFor each candidate in updates the network is trained, then its closed-loop forecast error over each window in windows is evaluated with loss.  The candidate with the lowest total loss is selected, and a final training pass is performed with it.\n\nFields:\n\nmethod: inner training method (e.g. TrainRecurrentNeuralNetwork);\nupdates: an UpdateRule iterable of hyper-parameter candidates;\nwindows: tuple of index ranges defining the validation forecast windows;\nloss: scoring function (true, predicted) -> Real (default log10RMSE).\n\nConstruct via RecycleValidation(method, ranges...; Nfolds, Ntrain, Nvalidation, loss).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.RecycleValidation"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.RidgeRegression","page":"Home","text":"struct RidgeRegression <: GridapType\n\nTikhonov-regularised (ridge) least-squares solver.\n\nSolves min_X AX - B^2 + lambda X^2 via the normal equations (A A^top + lambda I) X = A B^top.  The regularisation parameter λ is stored as a mutable reference so it can be updated by RecycleValidation during hyper-parameter search.\n\nConstruct via RidgeRegression(λ::Real).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.RidgeRegression"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.SecondMoment","page":"Home","text":"const SecondMoment = Law{2}\n\nType reserved for distributions characterised by their first two moments, i.e. mean and covariance, accessed via the functions mean and cov.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.SecondMoment"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.SecondOrderResultsTable","page":"Home","text":"mutable struct SecondOrderResultsTable <: ResultsTable\n  innovation_means::AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Real}}\n  innovation_stds::AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Real}}\n  innovation_nis::AbstractVector{<:Real}\n  innovation_rmse::AbstractVector{<:Real}\nend\n\nStores innovation diagnostics collected step-by-step during loop. Access via result.table on the returned FilterResults.\n\nFields:\n\ninnovation_means: mean innovation vector at each step (length-m vectors)\ninnovation_stds: diagonal std of the observation prior at each step\ninnovation_nis: Normalized Innovation Squared at each step\ninnovation_rmse: RMS of the mean innovation at each step\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.SecondOrderResultsTable"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.SigmaPoints","page":"Home","text":"struct SigmaPoints{A<:AbstractVector,B<:AbstractMatrix,C<:AbstractMatrix,D<:AbstractVector,E<:Real} <: SecondMoment\n  mean::A \n  covariance::B\n  points::C \n  weights_mean::D \n  weights_cov::D\n  λ::E\nend\n\nThis SecondMoment distribution represents the sigma points needed to run the UnscentedTransform. Fields:\n\npoints: n  (2*L + 1)-dimensional matrix storing the values of the sigma points;\nmean: n-dimensional vector representing the weighted mean of points;\ncovariance: n  n-dimensional matrix representing the weighted covariance of points;\nweights_mean: (2*L + 1)-dimensional vector storing weights for mean;\nweights_cov: (2*L + 1)-dimensional vector storing weights for covariance;\nλ: real value used in the update of the sigma points points.\n\nIn an Unscented Transformation, two things occur in an iterative fashion:\n\nthe field points is updated in-place via a call to sigma_points!;\nthe fields mean and covariance are updated in-place via a call to update!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.SigmaPoints"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.TaperModel","page":"Home","text":"struct TaperModel <: NonlinearModel\n\nApplies element-wise covariance localisation to a sample covariance or ensemble anomaly.\n\nGiven a covariance matrix Sigma, the localised matrix is\n\nSigma_ij^textloc = fleft(d_ijrright) cdot Sigma_ij\n\nwhere f is a TaperFunction, d_ij is the pre-computed distance between indices i and j, and r is the localisation radius (mutable, may be optimised).\n\nThe result is projected back to the nearest positive-semidefinite matrix via SVD to ensure it remains a valid covariance.\n\nFields:\n\ntaper: a TaperFunction instance;\ndistance: symmetric matrix of pairwise distances;\nradius: mutable reference to the localisation radius.\n\nConstruct via TaperModel(npoints; taper=GaspariCohn(), distance=ℓ2, radius=1.0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.TaperModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.ThreeDVar","page":"Home","text":"struct ThreeDVar <: DVar\n\nThree-Dimensional Variational data assimilation (3DVar).\n\nFinds the analysis state x^a that minimises the cost function\n\nJ(x) = fracalpha2(x - x^b)^top B^-1(x - x^b)\n      + fracbeta2(mathcalH(x) - y)^top R^-1(mathcalH(x) - y)\n\nwhere x^b is the background state, B and R are the background- and observation-error covariance matrices, and mathcalH is the observation operator. Optimisation is performed with BFGS (via Optim.jl).\n\nConstruct via\n\nThreeDVar(transition, observation, prior; B=..., R=...)\n\nand run one analysis step with analyse! or full sequence with loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.ThreeDVar"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.TrainRecurrentNeuralNetwork","page":"Home","text":"struct TrainRecurrentNeuralNetwork <: TrainMethod\n\nTraining strategy for RecurrentNeuralNetwork subtypes.\n\nThe readout weights are fitted by ridge regression on the reservoir states collected during an open-loop run.  Optional data augmentation and regularisation are applied before fitting, and a washout period discards the initial transient.\n\nFields:\n\nsolver: a RidgeRegression solver (holds the Tikhonov parameter λ);\naugmentation: a DataAugmentation applied to inputs before training;\nregularisation: a DataRegularisation applied to reservoir states;\nwashout: number of initial steps to discard before regression.\n\nConstruct via TrainRecurrentNeuralNetwork(; augmentation=..., regularisation=..., washout=0, λ=1e-16).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.TrainRecurrentNeuralNetwork"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.TransformStyle","page":"Home","text":"abstract type TransformStyle end\n\nBase type for nonlinear input-transformation strategies within a Modifier. Concrete subtypes: NoTransformation, T₁, T₂, T₃.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.TransformStyle"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.TransientPDEModel","page":"Home","text":"struct TransientPDEModel{A<:ODESolution} <: DifferentialModel\n\nWraps a Gridap/GridapROMs ODESolution (a parametric or non-parametric PDE solution object) as a NonlinearModel.\n\nEach call to evaluate! advances the PDE solver by one time step, making it suitable as the transition model in a filter for PDE-governed dynamical systems. Construct via Model(sol::ODEParamSolution).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.TransientPDEModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.T₁","page":"Home","text":"struct T₁ <: TransformStyle end\n\nTransformation that squares odd-indexed components: x[i] = i odd ? x[i]^2 : x[i].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.T₁"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.T₂","page":"Home","text":"struct T₂ <: TransformStyle end\n\nTransformation that replaces odd-indexed components (after the first) with the product of the two preceding components: x[i] = i > 1 && i odd ? x[i-1]*x[i-2] : x[i].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.T₂"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.T₃","page":"Home","text":"struct T₃ <: TransformStyle end\n\nTransformation that replaces interior odd-indexed components with the product of their neighbours: x[i] = 1 < i < n && i odd ? x[i+1]*x[i-1] : x[i].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.T₃"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.UniformLaw","page":"Home","text":"struct UniformLaw{A<:AbstractVector,B<:AbstractMatrix} <: SecondMoment\n  mean::A \n  covariance::B\n  lower_bound::A\n  upper_bound::A\nend\n\nUniform distribution of mean mean and covariance covariance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.UniformLaw"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.UnscentedTransform","page":"Home","text":"struct UnscentedTransform{A<:Model,B<:Model,C<:Law,D<:Law,E<:Law,F<:Law} <: KalmanFilter\n\nImplements the unscented transform. This model is analogous to a Kalman filter procedure for nonlinear transition/observation models, handling nonlinearities via sigma points (see SigmaPoints). These are a set of interpolation points used to approximate the first and second moments of a transitioned/observed distribution via a weighted linear combination. In particular:\n\nfor the forecasting step: we update the sigma points from the analysed distribution of the previous iteration; evaluate the transition model at each sigma point; update mean and covariance via weighted linear combinations (see SigmaPoints);\nfor the analysis step: we evaluate the observation model at each sigma point; update mean and covariance; compute innovation, Kalman gain, and state posterior as in a standard Kalman filter.\n\nThe prior C and observation prior D must both be (or wrap) SigmaPoints. Construct via KalmanFilter(transition, observation, prior) with a SigmaPoints (or ConstrainedSigmaPoints) prior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.UnscentedTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"#MeteoModels.UpdateRule","page":"Home","text":"abstract type UpdateRule end\n\nBase type for hyper-parameter search strategies used by RecycleValidation. Concrete subtypes iterate over candidate parameter sets:\n\nUpdateRule(stencils...): grid-search over Cartesian product of ranges (NetworkUpdate);\nUpdateRule(tikhonov, ranges...): joint grid over ridge λ and network parameters (NetworkAndTikhonovUpdate).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.UpdateRule"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.DoNotModify-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":"DoNotModify() -> Modifier\n\nReturns the identity Modifier: no normalisation, no transformation, no bias.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.DoNotModify"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.InnovationACF-Tuple{MeteoModels.ResultsTable}","page":"Home","text":"InnovationACF(t::ResultsTable;maxlag=20) -> AbstractVector\n\nAutocorrelation function of the innovation-norm time series from table. Under a well-specified filter innovations should be white noise, so ACF[lag > 0] ≈ 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.InnovationACF"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.NEES-Tuple{AbstractVector, SecondMoment}","page":"Home","text":"NEES(true_values::AbstractVector,d::Law) -> Real\n\nNormalized Estimation Error Squared. Under a well-calibrated filter, the expected value is 1. Values > 1 indicate underestimated uncertainty; < 1 indicate overestimated uncertainty.\n\nNEES(true_values::AbstractMatrix,history::AbstractVector{<:Law}) -> AbstractVector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NEES"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.NIS-Tuple{AbstractVector, SecondMoment}","page":"Home","text":"NIS(true_values::AbstractVector,d::Law) -> Real\n\nNormalized Innovation Squared. Under a consistent filter, the expected value is 1.\n\nNIS(true_values::AbstractMatrix,history::AbstractVector{<:Law}) -> AbstractVector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NIS"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.NLL-Tuple{AbstractVector, SecondMoment}","page":"Home","text":"NLL(true_values::AbstractVector,d::Law) -> Real\n\nComputes the Negative Log Likelihood between the true data true_values and the first moment of  the distribution d.\n\nNLL(true_values::AbstractMatrix,history::AbstractVector{<:Law}) -> AbstractVector\n\nComputes the Negative Log Likelihood between the true data true_values and history, the historical  distributions obtained by running the Kalman iterations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NLL"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.NRMSE-Tuple{AbstractVector, Law}","page":"Home","text":"NRMSE(true_values::AbstractVector,d::Law) -> Real\n\nComputes the Normalised Root Mean Square Error between the true data true_values and the first moment of  the distribution d. This is equal to the Root Mean Square Error divided by the standard deviation of d. \n\nNRMSE(true_values::AbstractMatrix,history::AbstractVector{<:Law}) -> AbstractVector\n\nComputes the Normalised Root Mean Square Error between the true data true_values and history, the historical  distributions obtained by running the Kalman iterations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NRMSE"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.Noise-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Home","text":"Noise(Σ::AbstractMatrix) -> NormalLaw\n\nConstructs a zero-mean NormalLaw with covariance Σ.  Intended for specifying process noise theta and observation noise eta in a filter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.Noise"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.NovoaEchoStateNetwork-Tuple","page":"Home","text":"NovoaEchoStateNetwork(args...; kwargs...) -> EchoStateNetwork\n\nVariant of EchoStateNetwork using the sparse reservoir initialisation from Novoa et al., with one non-zero entry per row in weights_in.  Accepts the same arguments as EchoStateNetwork.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.NovoaEchoStateNetwork"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.RMSE-Tuple{AbstractVector, Law}","page":"Home","text":"RMSE(true_values::AbstractVector,d::Law) -> Real\n\nComputes the Root Mean Square Error between the true data true_values and the first moment of  the distribution d.\n\nRMSE(true_values::AbstractMatrix,history::AbstractVector{<:Law}) -> AbstractVector\n\nComputes the Root Mean Square Error between the true data true_values and history, the historical  distributions obtained by running the Kalman iterations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.RMSE"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.RankHistogram-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector{<:Ensemble}}","page":"Home","text":"RankHistogram(true_values::AbstractMatrix,history::AbstractVector{<:Ensemble}) -> AbstractVector\n\nRank (Talagrand) histogram. For each time step and state variable, counts the rank of the true value within the sorted ensemble. A flat histogram indicates a well-calibrated ensemble; U-shaped indicates underdispersion; dome-shaped indicates overdispersion. Returns a normalised frequency vector of length ne + 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.RankHistogram"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.SpreadSkillRatio-Tuple{AbstractVector, Law}","page":"Home","text":"SpreadSkillRatio(true_values::AbstractVector,d::Law) -> Real\n\nRatio of mean ensemble spread to RMSE. Values ≈ 1 indicate a well-calibrated ensemble. Values < 1 indicate underdispersion; > 1 indicate overdispersion.\n\nSpreadSkillRatio(true_values::AbstractMatrix,history::AbstractVector{<:Law}) -> AbstractVector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.SpreadSkillRatio"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.analyse!-Tuple{Law, Filter, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Home","text":"analyse!(posterior::Law,f::Filter,args...) -> Law\n\nIn-place execution of the analysis step of a Kalman filter algorithm. This step consists of the  following operations:\n\nRunning the observation model on the forecasted distribution (see observation!)\nComputing the innovation (see innovation!)\nComputing the Kalman gain (see kalman_gain!)\nUpdating the forecasted distribution by accounting for the Kalman gain (see update!)\n\nTo run an iteration of the Kalman filter, one must run the forecasting step in forecast! prior to the analysis one. If no optional argument is provided, the analysis is not performed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.analyse!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.blockdiag-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}}","page":"Home","text":"blockdiag(A::AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix{T}}) where T -> BlockMatrix{T}\nblockdiag(A::AbstractMatrix{T}...) where T -> BlockMatrix{T}\n\nConstruct a BlockMatrix with A... on the diagonal and zeros elsewhere. All off-diagonal blocks exist and are filled with zeros (not empty).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.blockdiag"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.build_3d_observations-Tuple{Model, AbstractArray, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Home","text":"build_3d_observations(\n  a::Model,\n  x::AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix},\n  [obs_noise::Law],\n  args::Function...\n) -> AbstractArray{T,3}\n\nLike build_observations but for ensemble trajectories: x is a vector of ensemble matrices (one per time step), and the output is a 3-D array (m × ne × T) where ne is the ensemble size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.build_3d_observations"},{"category":"function","location":"#MeteoModels.build_linear_observation_model","page":"Home","text":"build_linear_observation_model(\n  ids::AbstractVector,\n  obs_ids::AbstractVector = ids;\n  start = 1\n) -> AlgebraicModel\n\nBuilds a linear observation model (selection matrix H) that extracts the components at indices obs_ids from a state vector of length length(ids).\n\nstart offsets all indices (useful when the state is a sub-block of a larger vector). Returns an AlgebraicModel wrapping the sparse (length(obs_ids) × length(ids)) matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.build_linear_observation_model"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.build_observations-Tuple{Model, AbstractArray, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Home","text":"build_observations(\n  a::Model,\n  x::AbstractMatrix,\n  [obs_noise::Law],\n  args::Function...\n) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nApplies the observation model a column-by-column to the state matrix x (each column is one time step) and collects the results into an (m × T) observation matrix, where m = dimension(a(x[:,1])) and T = size(x, 2).\n\nOptional positional Function arguments b are called as b(x[:,k]) and their output is added to the observation at step k (useful for adding a bias term).  If obs_noise::Law is provided, a random draw from it is added to every column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.build_observations"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.build_prior-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Number}}","page":"Home","text":"build_prior(state::AbstractVector; kwargs...) -> FirstMoment\nbuild_prior(states::AbstractMatrix; nsamples=1, kwargs...) -> Ensemble\nbuild_prior(state, noise::SecondMoment; nsamples=1, kwargs...) -> SecondMoment or Ensemble\nbuild_prior(states, c::AbstractConstraint; kwargs...) -> ConstrainedLaw\n\nConstructs the initial prior distribution from a state snapshot or ensemble matrix.\n\nA Vector with no noise returns a FirstMoment;\na Matrix returns an Ensemble;\nadding a noise::SecondMoment draws nsamples perturbations and sets the covariance;\nadding a constraint c wraps the result in a ConstrainedLaw.\n\nBlockVector/BlockMatrix inputs produce block-structured joint distributions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.build_prior"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.build_train_target_data-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"build_train_target_data(\n  true_data::AbstractMatrix,\n  data::AbstractMatrix\n) -> (train_data, target_data)\n\nbuild_train_target_data(\n  true_data::AbstractMatrix,\n  data::AbstractArray{<:Number,3}\n) -> (train_data, target_data)\n\nConstructs innovation-based training and target arrays for reservoir / neural-network bias correction.\n\nEach column of train_data[:,j] is true_data[:,j] - data[:,j] (the innovation at step j) and target_data[:,j] is the innovation at the next step j+1.  The 3-D overload handles ensemble data (shape (state_dim × ensemble × time)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.build_train_target_data"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.dimension-Tuple{Law}","page":"Home","text":"dimension(d::Law) -> Int\n\nDimension of the (vector) space on which the distribution is defined. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.dimension"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.draw-Tuple{SecondMoment}","page":"Home","text":"draw(d::SecondMoment;γ=1.0) -> AbstractVector \ndraw(d::SecondMoment,nsamples::Int;γ=1.0) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nDraws a n-dimensional random vector from the distribution d, where n represents the  dimension of d (see distribution). If an integer nsamples is also provided, the output  will be an n  nsamples - dimensional matrix. γ represents a scaling factor for the draw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.draw"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.execute-Tuple{Model, Law, AbstractVector}","page":"Home","text":"execute(a::Model, prior::Law, stencil::AbstractVector) -> AbstractVector{<:Law}\nexecute(f::Filter, stencil::AbstractVector) -> AbstractVector{<:Law}\n\nRuns the model a (or the transition model of filter f) forward for length(stencil) steps starting from prior, recording a copy of the distribution at each step. Returns the history as a vector of distributions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.execute"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.forecast!-Tuple{Law, Filter}","page":"Home","text":"forecast!(posterior::Law,f::Filter) -> Law\n\nIn-place execution of the forecast step of a Kalman filter algorithm. This step consists of the  following operations:\n\nRunning the transition model on the prior distribution (see transition!)\n\nTo complete a single iteration of the Kalman filter, one must run the analysis step in analyse! following the forecast one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.forecast!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.forecast-Tuple{NeuralNetwork, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Home","text":"forecast(network::NeuralNetwork, stencil; kwargs...) -> AbstractArray\n\nRuns network in closed-loop (autoregressive) mode for length(stencil) steps: the output at each step is fed back as the input for the next.  Returns the sequence of outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.forecast"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.forecasted_history-Tuple","page":"Home","text":"forecasted_history(args...) -> AbstractVector{<:Law}\n\nAlias for execute.  Runs the model forward and returns the full history of distributions.  Accepts the same arguments as execute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.forecasted_history"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.forecasted_law-Tuple","page":"Home","text":"forecasted_law(args...) -> Law\n\nReturns only the final distribution from forecasted_history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.forecasted_law"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.geostrophic-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"geostrophic(n::Int) -> AbstractMatrix\ngeostrophic(n::Dims{2}) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nReturns the pairwise Euclidean distance matrix for a 2-D Cartesian grid of shape (n, n) (or n when given as a Dims{2} tuple), laid out in column-major order. Intended for geophysical applications where the state is defined on a regular 2-D domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.geostrophic"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.get_noise-Tuple{Filter}","page":"Home","text":"get_noise(f::Filter) -> Law\n\nFetches the transition noise from the filter f. This noise, denoted by θ, is such that\n\nxₙ₁ = F(xₙ) + θ\n\nwhere xₖₖ is the state process, and F is the transition model. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.get_noise"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.get_observation_model-Tuple{Filter}","page":"Home","text":"get_observation_model(f::Filter) -> Model\n\nFetches the observation model from the filter f. This model, denoted by H, is such that\n\nyₙ = H(xₙ) + η\n\nwhere xₖₖ is the state process, yₖₖ is the observed process, and η is a (usually Gaussian)  random variable. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.get_observation_model"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.get_observation_noise-Tuple{Filter}","page":"Home","text":"get_observation_noise(f::Filter) -> Law\n\nFetches the observation noise from the filter f. This noise, denoted by η, is such that\n\nyₙ = H(xₙ) + η\n\nwhere xₖₖ is the state process, yₖₖ is the observed process, and H is the observation model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.get_observation_noise"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.get_observation_prior-Tuple{Filter}","page":"Home","text":"get_observation_prior(f::Filter) -> Law\n\nFetches the distribution of the observation variable from the filter f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.get_observation_prior"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.get_prior-Tuple{Filter}","page":"Home","text":"get_prior(f::Filter) -> Law\n\nFetches the distribution of the state variable from the filter f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.get_prior"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.get_transition_model-Tuple{Filter}","page":"Home","text":"get_transition_model(f::Filter) -> Model\n\nFetches the transition model from the filter f. This model, denoted by F, is such that\n\nxₙ₁ = F(xₙ) + θ\n\nwhere xₖₖ is the state process, and θ is a (usually Gaussian) random variable. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.get_transition_model"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.identify_parameter-Tuple{ADParamIdentification, AbstractVector}","page":"Home","text":"identify_parameter(\n  ad::ADParamIdentification,\n  obs::AbstractVector;\n  μ0 = sample_number(ad.pspace),\n  iterations = 1000,\n  x_abstol = 1e-12,\n  x_reltol = 1e-6,\n  show_trace = true,\n  kwargs...\n) -> Optim.OptimizationResults\n\nIdentify the parameter vector μ that best explains observations obs by minimising the weighted least-squares loss\n\nell(mu) = R^-12(mathcalH(u(mu)) - y)_Omega^2\n\nwhere u(mu) solves the parametric PDE, mathcalH is the observation operator (ad.u_to_obs), and the norm is the finite-element L^2(Omega) norm.\n\nGradients are obtained via Zygote AD, which differentiates through the custom rrules on AffineFEStateMap, StateParamMap, and LinearModel. Optimisation uses Fminbox(LBFGS()) with box constraints from ad.pspace.\n\nArguments\n\nobs: observed data in the observation space;\nμ0: initial guess (defaults to a Halton sample from ad.pspace);\niterations, x_abstol, x_reltol, show_trace: forwarded to Optim.Options;\nkwargs...: additional keyword arguments forwarded to Optim.Options.\n\nReturns an Optim.OptimizationResults; use Optim.minimizer to extract the solution and Optim.converged to check convergence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.identify_parameter"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.innovation!-Tuple{Filter, Law, Union{Number, AbstractArray{<:Number}}}","page":"Home","text":"innovation!(f::Filter,posterior::Law,z::InType) -> InType\n\nGiven a distribution posterior and an observation z, returns the innovation ỹ such that\n\nỹ = z - yₙ = z - H(xᶠₙ) + η\n\nwhere yₙ represents the observation forecasted by the filter f, and xᶠₙ is distributed according  to posterior. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.innovation!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.jac-Tuple{Union{Gridap.Arrays.Map, Function}, Any}","page":"Home","text":"jac(a::Union{Function,Map},x) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nReturns a's Jacobian matrix evaluated in x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.jac"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.joint_law-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"joint_law(d::AbstractVector{<:Law}) -> Law\n\nGiven a list of marginal distributions d = (d1,...,dn), returns their joint distribution. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.joint_law"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.kalman_gain!-Tuple{Filter, Law}","page":"Home","text":"kalman_gain!(f::Filter,posterior::Law) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nIn-place computation of the Kalman gain K according to the formula\n\nK = Σxy  Σy¹\n\nwhere Σxy and Σy are the state-observation and observation covariance matrices, respectively. K is storede as a cached object in f, while the covariances Σxy and Σy can be computed  by suitably accessing the transition and observation distributions via get_prior and  get_observation_prior, respectively.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.kalman_gain!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.linearise-Tuple{Model, Union{Number, AbstractArray{<:Number}}}","page":"Home","text":"linearise(a::Model,x::InType) -> LinearModel\n\nLinearizes a model a around x. If a is a LinearModel, it returns a itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.linearise"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.loop-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Filter, AbstractArray{T, N}}} where {T, N}","page":"Home","text":"loop(f::Filter,obs::AbstractArray) -> FilterResults\n\nGiven a filter f and observations obs, iteratively runs the forecast-analyse paradigm typical of a Kalman filter, producing a FilterResults that acts as a vector of posterior distributions and also carries a ResultsTable of innovation diagnostics.\n\nIf a slice of obs along its last dimension is all-NaN, the analysis step is skipped (forecast only) and NaN entries are pushed to the table. Use expand to embed sparse observations into a fine grid before passing to loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.loop"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.mixed_cov!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, Filter, Law}","page":"Home","text":"mixed_cov!(Σ::AbstractMatrix,f::Filter,posterior::Law) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nIn-place computation of the state-observation \"mixed\" covariance Σ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.mixed_cov!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.mixed_cov!-Tuple{Any, SigmaPoints, SigmaPoints}","page":"Home","text":"mixed_cov!(cache,a::SigmaPoints,b::SigmaPoints) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nIn-place computation of the covariance between the SigmaPoints distributions a and b.  These two distributions should have the same L (i.e. the same number of sigma points) and λ parameters, which also implies that they share the same mean/covariance weights. The formula used here is: \n\nΣ = ᵢ₁²ᴸ¹ wi  (χᵃi - μᵃ)  (χᵇi - μᵇ)\n\nwhere χᵃ and μᵃ are the sigma points and their mean for a, χᵇ and μᵇ are the sigma points  and their mean for b, and w are the covariance weights of either a or b.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.mixed_cov!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.observation!-Tuple{Filter, Law}","page":"Home","text":"observation!(f::Filter,posterior::Law) -> Law\n\nIn-place application of the observation model stored in f on the distribution posterior, which represents the posterior distribution of the state variable. In essence, denoting by F the transition model, if posterior represents the forecasted distribution of the state variable  xᶠₙ at the nth iteration, this step runs the observation model\n\nyₙ = H(xᶠₙ) + η\n\noverwriting the result yₙ in the distribution of the observed variable stored in f, and accessible through get_observation_prior. This function should be run during the analysis step  in a Kalman filter algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.observation!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.observe-Tuple{Model, Law}","page":"Home","text":"observe(a::Model, d::Law) -> AbstractArray\n\nApplies the model a to the state (mean or ensemble matrix) of the distribution d and returns the result without modifying d.  Equivalent to a(get_state(d)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.observe"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.predicted_history-Tuple","page":"Home","text":"predicted_history(args...) -> FilterResults\n\nAlias for loop.  Runs the filter forward and returns the full assimilation history (posteriors + innovation table).  Accepts the same arguments as loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.predicted_history"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.predicted_law-Tuple","page":"Home","text":"predicted_law(args...) -> Law\n\nReturns only the final posterior distribution from predicted_history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.predicted_law"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.sigma_points!-Tuple{SigmaPoints, SigmaPoints}","page":"Home","text":"sigma_points!(dcache::SigmaPoints,d::SigmaPoints;kwargs...) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nIn-place update of the sigma points according to the formula:\n\nχₖ₁1 = μₖ\nχₖ₁2L+1 = μₖ + ((L + λ)Pₖ)\nχₖ₁L+22L+1 = μₖ - ((L + λ)Pₖ)\n\nwhere μₖ and Pₖ are the previous mean and covariance fields. The output χₖ₁ overwrites the  field points.  \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.sigma_points!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.sigma_points-Tuple{SecondMoment}","page":"Home","text":"sigma_points(d::SecondMoment;kwargs...) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nGiven an input distribution d, computes the sigma points χ according to the formula:\n\nχ1 = μ\nχ2L+1 = μ + ((L + λ)Σ)\nχL+22L+1 = μ - ((L + λ)Σ)\n\nwhere μ and Σ are the mean and covariance of d, respectively. The variables L and λ may  be passed as keyword arguments, and assume the following default values:\n\nL = dimension(d)\nλ = 3 - L\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.sigma_points"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.smooth_loop-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Filter, AbstractArray{T, N}, Vararg{Any}}} where {T, N}","page":"Home","text":"smooth_loop(f::Filter, obs::AbstractArray; smoother=RTS()) -> FilterResults\n\nCombined forward-filter and backward-smoother pass.\n\nRuns loop on f with observations obs, then immediately applies smoothen! to refine the history.  Returns a FilterResults whose state_history contains the smoothed posteriors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.smooth_loop"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.smoothen!-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Law}, MeteoModels.Smoother, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Home","text":"smoothen!(\n  smooth_history::AbstractVector{<:Law},\n  smoother::Smoother,\n  filter::KalmanFilter,\n  history::AbstractVector{<:Law}\n)\n\nIn-place backward smoothing pass.  Given the forward-filter posteriors history (from loop), overwrites smooth_history with the smoothed distributions produced by smoother.\n\nThe two-argument form smoothen!(smooth_history, filter, history; smoother=RTS()) also works and defaults to the RTS smoother.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.smoothen!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.transition!-Tuple{Law, Filter}","page":"Home","text":"transition!(posterior::Law,f::Filter) -> Law\n\nIn-place application of the transition model stored in f on the distribution posterior, which represents the posterior distribution of the state variable. In essence, denoting by F the transition model, if posterior represents the distribution of the state variable xₙ at the  nth iteration, this step runs the transition model\n\nxₙ₁ = F(xₙ) + θ\n\noverwriting the result xₙ₁ in posterior. This function should be run during the forecast  step in a Kalman filter algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.transition!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.update!-Tuple{Any, SigmaPoints}","page":"Home","text":"update!(cache,d::SigmaPoints) -> SigmaPoints\n\nUpdate the mean and covariance of the sigma points d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.update!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.update!-Tuple{Ensemble}","page":"Home","text":"update!(d::Ensemble) -> Ensemble\n\nUpdate the mean and anomaly of the ensemble d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.update!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.update!-Tuple{Law, Filter}","page":"Home","text":"update!(posterior::Law,f::Filter) -> Law\n\nIn-place update of the distribution posterior through the action of Kalman gain matrix cached  in f. Denoting by K the Kalman gain computed by running kalman_gain!, and by ỹ the innovation computed by running innovation!, if posterior represents the forecasted  distribution of the state variable xᶠₙ at the nth iteration, this step runs the formula\n\nxᵃₙ = xᶠₙ + K  ỹ\n\nand overwrites the analysed distribution of the state variable xᵃₙ in posterior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.update!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.update_table!-Tuple{MeteoModels.FirstOrderResultsTable, Filter, Any}","page":"Home","text":"update_table!(table::ResultsTable,f::Filter)\n\nExtracts the current innovation and observation-prior std from f and appends NIS, RMSE, mean and std to table. Called automatically inside loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.update_table!"},{"category":"function","location":"#MeteoModels.visualise","page":"Home","text":"visualise(\n  history::AbstractVector{<:Law},\n  grid=eachindex(history);\n  variable::Int=1,\n  kwargs...\n)\n\nvisualise(\n  true_values::AbstractMatrix,\n  history::AbstractVector{<:Law},\n  grid=eachindex(history);\n  variable::Int=1,\n  kwargs...\n)\n\nPlot the historical distributions obtained by running the Kalman iterations, for e.g. via the  function loop. The primary estimator is the mean of the distributions. If the distributions  feature a second moment (i.e. they are equipped by a variance) then a confidence interval is also drawn. The true data true_values, if known, may be provided, and will be plotted on the same figure. The  keyword variable – an integer – indicates the state member to be plotted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.visualise"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.warmup!-Tuple{Filter, AbstractVector}","page":"Home","text":"warmup!(f::Filter, stencil::AbstractVector)\nwarmup!(a::MemoryModel, prior::Law, stencil::AbstractVector)\n\nAdvances the model or filter forward for length(stencil) steps, updating the internal state in-place without recording the history.  Used to spin up memory-based models (e.g. EchoStateNetwork, ODEModel) before the assimilation window.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.warmup!"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.ℓ1-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"ℓ1(n::Int) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nReturns the n times n periodic ell^1 (Manhattan) distance matrix for a one-dimensional grid of n equally-spaced points, with wrap-around at the boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.ℓ1"},{"category":"method","location":"#MeteoModels.ℓ2-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"ℓ2(n::Int) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nReturns the n times n Euclidean (ell^2) distance matrix for a one-dimensional grid of n equally-spaced points.  Also available as the alias euclidean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeteoModels.ℓ2"},{"category":"method","location":"#ReservoirComputing.train-Tuple{MeteoModels.TrainMethod, NeuralNetwork, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Home","text":"train(method::TrainMethod, network::NeuralNetwork, x, y; kwargs...) -> states\n\nFits network on training data (x, y) using method.  Allocates the training cache, runs the open-loop pass, and solves for the readout weights in-place.  Returns the collected reservoir states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReservoirComputing.train"}]
}
